AML is as follows:\n\n``` php\narray(\n    'parameters' => array(\n        array(\n            'name' => 'name1', \n            'value' => 'value1'\n        ),\n        array(\n            'name' => 'name2',\n            'value' => 'value2'\n        )\n    )\n)\n```\n\nIf the 'parameters' node is defined as a prototyped array and its key attribute is 'name', then the data will be changed to:\n\n``` php\narray(\n    'parameters' => array(\n        'name1' => array(\n            'value' => 'value1'\n        ),\n        'name2' => array(\n            'value' => 'value2'\n        )\n    )\n)\n```\n\nNow, because there is only one key named 'value' left in its child nodes, which is a special name as per the specification, the data is supposed to be further simplified to:\n\n``` php\narray(\n    'parameters' => array(\n        'name1' => 'value1', \n        'name2' => 'value2'\n    )\n)\n```\n\nBut, if you try the example code I provided, an `Symfony\\Component\\Config\\Definition\\Exception\\InvalidTypeException` is thrown.\n\nI have addressed the root cause of this issue:\n1) The original prototype of node 'parameters' is an array node, which matches the type of its child nodes:\n\n``` php\narray(\n    'parameters' => array(\n        array( // child node is array, prototype is array too\n            'name' => 'name1', \n            'value' => 'value1'\n        ),\n        ...\n    )\n)\n```\n\n2) After the data structure is simplified, the type of its child nodes is changed to scalar, but the prototype is still an array node:\n\n``` php\narray(\n    'parameters' => array(\n        'name' => 'value1', // child node is scalar, but prototype is array\n        ...\n    )\n)\n```\n","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/symfony/symfony/issues/comments/124914923/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"log